"This surah begins with the expression, \"Indeed, We sent it down.\" According to the majority, the pronoun \"it\" here refers to the Quran. The greatness of the One who sent it down and the glory of what was sent down are emphasized. Although some commentators have stated that the pronoun refers to the command \"read\" in Surah al-Alaq or to the word \"sef'\" pointing to the victory of Badr, the strongest view is that it refers to the Quran. This verse informs that the Quran was sent down on the Night of Qadr (Laylat al-Qadr).\n\nThe Night of Qadr carries the meanings of \"the night of decree\", \"the night of honor\", or \"the night of constriction\". As stated in Surah ad-Dukhan, every wise matter is distinguished on this night. All three of these meanings are valid, and the mention of \"the Night of Qadr\" three times in the surah points to this.\n\n\"The Night of Qadr is better than a thousand months.\" This expression conveys that deeds performed on that night are more virtuous than the deeds of a thousand months that do not contain the Night of Qadr. A thousand months equals eighty-three years and four months. In some narrations, this has been interpreted as referring to the thousand months of worship of a person from the Children of Israel, or to the duration of the Umayyad state. However, the essential meaning is to emphasize the boundless goodness of this night.\n\n\"The angels and the Spirit (Gabriel) descend therein by permission of their Lord for every matter.\" According to the majority, what is meant by the Spirit is Gabriel (Jibril). The descent is to the lowest heaven or to the earth. The expression \"for every matter\" encompasses all affairs that are decreed or to be executed on that night.\n\n\"That night is peace until the break of dawn.\" Salam signifies the angels greeting the believers, protection from evil, or complete peace and tranquility. Supplicating on this night is a Sunnah; the supplication taught to Aisha is: \"O Allah, You are Pardoning, You love to pardon, so pardon me.\" Prayer, reciting the Quran, and contemplation are also recommended.\n\nThe Night of Qadr is likely to occur concealed each year in Ramadan, especially in the last ten days, and most probably on the twenty-seventh night. Although some have said that it was a one-time event because it was the night when the first revelation descended or when the victory of Badr occurred, the majority hold the view that it recurs every year. Whoever spends this night in worship attains a reward better than a thousand months. In connection with the command \"read\" in the preceding surah, the surah counsels prostrating and worshipping Allah, who sent down this magnificent Book."